//! LiberSystem memory balloon - the contract a `memory-balloon` provider serves: what a virtual machine's host asks to
//! have back, and the allowance ResourceManager grants against it.
//!
//! THE HOST ASKS AND THE MACHINE DECIDES. The host sets a target - how many pages it wants back - and the balloon holds
//! the smaller of that and the allowance its one consumer, ResourceManager, grants; the kernel adds its own floor under
//! both. Only FREE pages are ever given - never one a process, a buffer or a device holds - so no Domain is ever made to
//! give anything up for the host, and when the machine needs memory again its policy lowers the allowance and the
//! balloon gives pages back to it first.
//!
//! IN PAGES OF 4 KiB, the unit the device counts in.

package liber:balloon-device@1;

use liber:base@1.{error};

/// Where the balloon stands.
record balloon-status {
	/// What the host asks this machine to give back.
	target:    u64,
	/// What the balloon holds now.
	held:      u64,
	/// The most the policy allows it to hold.
	allowed:   u64,
	/// How much of the target the allowance or the kernel's floor holds back: what the host asked for and is not given.
	held-back: u64,
}

/// THE CONTRACT a `memory-balloon` publication serves.
interface balloon-device {
	/// Where the balloon stands.
	@op(1) status: func() -> result<balloon-status, error>;
	/// The most it may hold from now on. It moves toward the smaller of the host's target and this in bounded batches,
	/// and gives pages back first when this falls below what it holds.
	@op(2) allow:  func(pages: u64) -> result<balloon-status, error>;
}
